# Public discoverability and crawler access (all agents)

Applies when adding or editing any public page, public route handler, public content file,
structured data, sitemap route, robots policy, footer/nav link, or marketing copy that search
engines or answer engines may crawl.

Use together with `docs/agent_rules/public-page-seo-metadata.md`. That rule owns title,
description, and canonical length gates; this rule owns crawlability, indexability, structured
content, internal linking, and AI/search crawler readability.

## Crawlability and indexing

- Public pages must return HTTP 200 without auth, geo, cookie, modal, or JavaScript-only gates.
- Do not add `noindex`, `nofollow`, `X-Robots-Tag`, auth redirects, middleware blocks, or
  `robots.txt` disallows to a public route unless the human explicitly says the page must not
  be indexed.
- Keep private app routes disallowed: `/admin/`, `/auth/`, `/dashboard/`, `/screening/`,
  `/settings/`, `/training/`, `/kyc/`, `/clients`, `/reports`, and equivalent authenticated
  areas.
- Keep `robots.txt` pointing to `https://yourapp.com.au/sitemap.xml`.
- Explicitly allow search and AI **retrieval/citation** crawlers used by priority engines when the
  intent is public discoverability: `bingbot`, `DuckDuckBot`, `DuckAssistBot`, `ChatGPT-User`,
  `OAI-SearchBot`, `anthropic-ai`, `Claude-User`, `Claude-SearchBot`, `PerplexityBot`,
  `Perplexity-User`, and `Google-Agent`. These fetch content to answer a user's question and cite
  the source, so discovery and traffic flow back.
  `DuckAssistBot` (DuckDuckGo's AI-answer crawler) is a distinct UA from `DuckDuckBot` (classic
  search indexing) — added 17 Aug 2026 after a Cloudflare AI-crawler report showed it missing from
  the explicit list (it wasn't actually blocked, since it fell through to the wildcard `allow: '/'`
  rule, but the explicit list should name every welcomed engine per this rule's own intent).
  `Perplexity-User` (Perplexity's real-time fetch agent, distinct from indexing-only
  `PerplexityBot`) and `Google-Agent` (Gemini's real-time user-triggered fetcher for live
  browsing) were added the same day for the same reason.
- Explicitly **disallow** bulk training / data-harvesting crawlers, which ingest content for model
  training or resale with no retrieval, citation, or traffic back: `GPTBot`, `CCBot`, `Bytespider`,
  `Amazonbot`, `meta-externalagent`, `Applebot-Extended`, `cohere-ai`, `Diffbot`, `ClaudeBot`,
  `Google-Extended`.
  - **Human decision 2026-08-01:** `GPTBot` moved from the allow list to the disallow list. This
    rule previously mandated allowing it, which contradicted the `Content-Signal:
    search=yes, ai-train=no, use=reference` the site already publishes. `OAI-SearchBot` and
    `ChatGPT-User` are what keep YourApp citable inside ChatGPT — `GPTBot` only feeds training.
  - **Same mistake found and fixed for Anthropic, 17 Aug 2026:** `ClaudeBot` was wrongly in the
    allow list. Anthropic's own support docs are explicit that `ClaudeBot` is the *training*
    crawler ("collecting web content that could potentially contribute to their training"); the
    actual citation crawlers are `Claude-User` (real-time fetch when a Claude user asks a
    question) and `Claude-SearchBot` (search-quality indexing) — both now allowed instead.
    Verify against Anthropic's own crawler docs before assuming this list is still accurate; these
    UA names have moved before and will again.
  - **`Google-Extended` is a different case — human decision, not a bug fix, 17 Aug 2026.** Unlike
    OpenAI/Anthropic/Perplexity, Google gives no way to split Gemini training-use from
    grounding/citation-use of indexed content — `Google-Extended` is one combined toggle for both.
    Given the site's "no training, only answering" principle, it was moved to disallow; the real
    cost is that YourApp's content also stops grounding Gemini/Vertex AI answers, since that
    trade-off can't be avoided the way it can for the other three engines. `Google-Agent`
    (Gemini's separate real-time fetcher for live mid-conversation browsing) is unaffected and
    stays allowed — it's a different mechanism from the indexed-content toggle.
  - `Google-Extended` governs Gemini grounding/training only and `Applebot-Extended` governs Apple
    Intelligence training only. **Neither affects Google Search or Apple search ranking**, so the
    allow/disallow split above costs nothing in classic SEO.
  - `robots.txt` is advisory in both places — as of 2026-08-01 the `yourapp.com.au` zone has
    **no Cloudflare bot enforcement enabled** (`ai_bots_protection`, `ai_training`, `ai_search`,
    `ai_user`, `crawler_protection`, `content_bots_protection` all `disabled`). Do not assume the
    edge is blocking anything; check before claiming it.
  - Cloudflare's *managed* `robots.txt` (`is_robots_txt_managed`) injects its own block at the
    edge **ahead of** `frontend/app/robots.ts`, and its per-agent groups win over ours. It
    silently contradicted this repo's policy until it was disabled on 2026-08-01. If robots.txt
    output ever disagrees with `app/robots.ts`, check this flag first:

    ```bash
    # read  (GET works with the account token in yourapp/prod/backend)
    curl -s "https://api.cloudflare.com/client/v4/zones/$ZONE/bot_management" -H "Authorization: Bearer $T"
    # write — must be PUT; PATCH returns 10405 "Method not allowed for this authentication scheme"
    curl -s -X PUT ".../bot_management" -H ... --data '{"is_robots_txt_managed": false}'
    ```

    Disabling it also removes Cloudflare's `Content-Signal:` lines and their EU Copyright
    Directive Article 4 reservation-of-rights text. Next's `MetadataRoute.Robots` cannot emit
    arbitrary lines, so restoring those requires a custom route handler in place of
    `app/robots.ts`.

## Sitemap, canonicals, and URLs

- Register every indexable **static** public route in `frontend/lib/site.ts::sitemapRoutes`.
- **Exception — admin-published content (C33 marketing blog):** `/Compliance/compliance-blog/{slug}`
  routes are NOT registered individually in `sitemapRoutes`. `frontend/app/sitemap.ts` fetches the
  live published-posts index (`getPublishedBlogPosts()`) at sitemap-build time and generates one
  entry per slug with `lastModified` from that post's real `updated_at` — this is what lets an
  `AMLHIVE_ADMIN` publish a post with no engineer/deploy in the loop, per the C33 proposal. Only the
  list page itself (`/Compliance/compliance-blog`) stays in the static array. If you add another
  admin-published content type with the same "no deploy" model, follow this same pattern rather
  than hand-maintaining slugs in `site.ts`.
- Add a real, explicit `lastModified` entry in `frontend/app/sitemap.ts` for every **static**
  sitemap route; do not rely on `DEFAULT_LASTMOD` for newly added or recently changed pages.
- `lastModified` must match the content's real publish/update date, not today's date by habit.
- Use one clean canonical URL per page: no tracking parameters, no fragments, no duplicate
  slash variants, and no canonical-to-homepage for child pages.
- Add or update `frontend/lib/public-seo-metadata-registry.ts` and run the SEO metadata audit
  for every public HTML route.

## Content readability

- Put the primary answer in visible HTML: H1, intro paragraph, H2 sections, lists, tables,
  FAQs, and clear links. Do not hide core facts inside client-only state, image-only text,
  accordions without server-rendered content, or scripts only.
- Use crawlable `<a href>` links for navigation and related content. JavaScript-only click
  handlers are not enough for public discovery.
- Every new high-intent page needs at least one internal link from a relevant indexed page
  such as homepage, `/about`, `/austrac-compliance`, `/Compliance/compliance-blog`, or a
  related guide.
- Keep content current: no "coming soon" or countdown phrasing after obligations are in force;
  no stale dates; no infrastructure vendor claims that will become false during migration.

## Structured data

- JSON-LD must match visible page content. Never add facts to schema that users cannot also
  verify on the page.
- Keep sitewide `Organization`, `WebSite`, and `SoftwareApplication` JSON-LD consistent with
  `frontend/lib/site.ts`.
- Use `Article` JSON-LD for guides/blog posts with accurate `datePublished` and
  `dateModified`; update the sitemap `lastModified` when `dateModified` changes.
- Use `FAQPage` only when the exact questions and answers are visibly rendered on the page.
- Use public `sameAs` URLs only. Never include admin dashboards, private account URLs,
  staging URLs, or competitor URLs in YourApp entity schema.

## AI answer-engine files

- Update `/llms.txt` and `/llms-full.txt` when product positioning, pricing, public URLs,
  regulatory dates, or key boundaries change.
- Keep the independence statement, ABN, "No per-check fees", not-legal-advice boundary, and
  manual-lodgement boundary consistent across website copy and AI context files.
- Do not name competitors in public denial copy or AI context files unless a separate
  human-approved comparison-page OpenSpec exists.

## Verification

Run at least:

```bash
cd frontend && npx vitest run tests/unit/app/search-discovery.test.ts tests/unit/app/entity-identity.test.ts tests/unit/app/public-page-seo-metadata.test.ts tests/unit/lib/ai-discovery.test.ts tests/unit/app/llms-routes.test.ts
cd frontend && npm run audit:seo-metadata
```

For production-visible changes, also run the live audit after deploy:

```bash
cd frontend && npm run audit:seo-metadata -- --live --base-url=https://yourapp.com.au --blog-api-url=https://api.yourapp.com.au
```

## Source basis

This rule is grounded in Google Search Central guidance on crawlable, useful content, sitemaps,
structured data, and AI features; OpenAI crawler documentation for GPTBot, ChatGPT-User, and
OAI-SearchBot; Bing Webmaster/IndexNow guidance for faster URL discovery; and schema.org
definitions for Organization, SoftwareApplication, Article, and FAQPage.
